<Base
  title="Thesis"
  description="Why the Democratic AI catalogue exists: reciprocity, open weights, performance, research, and the commons."
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The argument</p>
      <h1>The commons is the input. Reciprocity is the test.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every capable model is built from human work: books, code, images, speech, conversation, and the record of
        everyone who came before. The question is not whether a model learned from the commons. It did. The question
        is what it gives back.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap prose" style="padding:2rem 0 3rem">
    <div class="panel">
      <h2>Open weights are a start, not the whole gift</h2>
      <p>
        A model is called <strong>open weight</strong> when its trained parameters are downloadable. You can run it,
        study its behaviour, fine-tune it, and build on it. That is a real return to the commons. It is not nothing,
        and this catalogue does not treat it as a failure.
      </p>
      <p>
        But open weights are not the same as open source. The four freedoms of open source ask for more: use,
        study, modification, and distribution. To study how a model was made, you need the training data and the
        training code. To reproduce it, you need the recipe. A release that publishes weights but keeps the data and
        code closed gives you a powerful artefact without the knowledge of how it came to be.
      </p>
      <p>
        The catalogue records the difference. <strong>Commons</strong> means weights, data, and code are open.
        <strong>Reciprocal</strong> means weights and code are open and the data is documented. <strong>Shared</strong>
        means the weights are available but the data stays closed or the licence restricts reuse. The tier is a
        description of the gift. It is not a grade of the model's worth.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h2>Piracy is not the same as liberation</h2>
      <p>
        Copyright law is often described as protection against loss of sale. That frame is too narrow. A shared film
        in a classroom, a quotation saved for study, or a cover played for friends is not the same act as building a
        commercial product on a corpus taken without consent. The harm is not only a lost sale. The harm is the
        broken reciprocity: value is taken from the commons and enclosed, while the people who made it receive
        nothing back.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is why the catalogue separates three questions:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Commons contribution.</strong> Did the lab publish new open knowledge, or only consume it?</li>
        <li><strong>Liberation valence.</strong> Did the lab break open enclosed capital and return it to the commons?</li>
        <li><strong>Third-party harm.</strong> Did the lab take from non-consenting people, scrape user data, bypass
          robots.txt, or lobby for artificial scarcity?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Distillation is not automatically theft. If a model was built on pirated books and then closed, distilling
        from it and releasing the result under a reuse-permitting licence can be restitution. If a lab distils from
        its own closed model and calls the result open, that is not restitution. The relevant questions are who the
        work came from, whether they consented, and what went back.
      </p>
      <p>
        The argument is not that every restriction is illegitimate. It is that property should serve equity,
        fairness, and citation, not only the protection of capital. A commons that is enclosed and rented back to
        its authors is not a market. It is a toll.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h2>Three independent judgements</h2>
      <p>
        The scorecard keeps three judgements apart, and adds a fourth signal. They measure different things, and a
        model can be excellent on one and poor on another.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Axis</th><th>Question</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Openness/Morality</strong></td><td>What did the release give back, and what did it take without consent?</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Performance/Intelligence</strong></td><td>How well does it do the job today, on a current primary benchmark?</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Research/Citation</strong></td><td>Can it find and attribute sources, and how often does it fabricate them?</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Syndication</strong></td><td>Have independent registries and aggregators noticed it? This is a signal, not a quality score.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The axes do not collapse into one ladder. A model can be open science and a weak coder. A model can be the
        best coder in the world and a poor citizen. A model can be fully open and unable to cite a source. A model
        can cite well through a vendor interface while its open weights cannot cite at all. The scorecard says which
        is which.
      </p>
      <p>
        The numbers are index scores, not percentages. They do not need to sum to 100. A missing score is not zero.
        When the evidence is absent, the card says so.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h2>Performance is not a licence</h2>
      <p>
        Capability is often used as a moral argument: a model is so useful that its terms, its data, or its conduct
        should be excused. This catalogue rejects that move. A strong model that takes without consent is still a
        strong model that takes without consent. A weak model that publishes its data, code, and recipe has still
        made a real contribution.
      </p>
      <p>
        Performance is measured on the job, not on the marketing. For coding models, the catalogue uses the primary
        agentic benchmarks: SWE-bench Verified, SWE-bench Pro, Terminal-Bench, and LiveCodeBench. For speech models,
        it uses average word error rate. For other models, it uses the best current primary benchmark. Aggregator
        rows, model-card figures, and base-model inheritance are labelled. A self-reported figure is marked. A
        missing figure stays missing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h2>Citations are part of the commons</h2>
      <p>
        A model that cannot show its sources asks the reader to trust it. A model that fabricates citations does
        worse: it borrows the appearance of evidence without the substance. Research and citation are therefore a
        separate axis, not a footnote to capability.
      </p>
      <p>
        The catalogue distinguishes three things: inline source attribution, web grounding, and citation
        hallucination. It also distinguishes the model from the vendor's chat product. Many open-weight models have
        a hosted chat interface with search and citations, while the weights themselves have neither. The score
        records what the model can do, not what a proprietary wrapper can do around it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h2>A living reference, not a frozen essay</h2>
      <p>
        Static comparisons age quickly. A table written in July can be wrong by October: models are renamed, weights
        are released late, benchmarks are revised, and licences change. This site is the living version. Every
        record carries its sources and the date of the last check. Every score can be traced to a benchmark, a
        model card, or a release term.
      </p>
      <p>
        The catalogue is also a correction machine. If a model is missing, if a licence is misread, if a benchmark
        has a newer result, open an issue with the source. The record changes. The argument stays open to evidence.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h2>What we ask of a release</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Publish the weights</strong> under a licence that permits reuse.</li>
        <li><strong>Publish the training data</strong>, or document it well enough to study.</li>
        <li><strong>Publish the training code</strong> and the evaluation recipe.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not take non-consenting work</strong> and call it innovation.</li>
        <li><strong>Cite sources</strong> in the product, not only in the launch post.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep the record current.</strong> A release is a promise that has to be maintained.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        That is the democratic AI test. Not whether a model is useful. It is whether the value it takes from the
        commons returns to the commons.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
